messages:
maya: quick question — can we split aggregate routing (tiling/mux) from RBAC evaluation so queries to dashboards avoid the authz hotpath? seeing tail latency spikes when authz service flaps.

sam: probably. two main options: pushdown authz into projection layer (filter early) or do routing first then run authz per-tenant in parallel. tradeoffs: cacheability vs correctness and auditability.

irene: FYI our SLO dashboards show authz adds ~30–120ms p95 for multi-tenant fanout during busy periods.

alex: experiment idea: allow a fast-path that uses cached allow-lists and marks results as `stale_auth`; if a strict check is required, fallback to strict path. canary behind a feature flag.

luca: concern: cached allow-lists open a window for permission changes. how long is TTL? we could wire token/role-change hooks to invalidate.

maya: TTL today is 60s. token revocations are rare but we should support immediate invalidation on role-change events.

sam: infra notes: if we decouple routing we must preserve request-scoped tracing IDs so we can correlate shards to final auth decisions for audit and debugging. also propagate an `auth_version` header.

irene: perf tradeoff: routing-first avoids blocking on an auth service call for every shard which reduces tail latency for read-heavy dashboards; writes and admin endpoints still need strict path.

alex: concrete proposal:
1) implement routing-first with cached allow-lists and an `auth_hint` header;
2) automatic fallback to strict eval on mismatch or when allow-list is missing;
3) rollout behind a flag with canary at 5% tenants;
4) emit audit stamps + tracing for every fallback.

maya: +1. let's sketch the RPC surface and headers so eng-runtime can wire it.

sam: interface sketch:
```proto
service QueryRouter {
  rpc Route(QueryRequest) returns (RoutePlan) {
    option (x-routing-version) = "v2";
  }
}

message QueryRequest {
  string tenant_id = 1;
  bytes query = 2;
  map<string,string> hints = 3; // auth_hint, auth_version
}
```

alex: add metrics: `router.auth_hint_hit`, `router.fallback_to_strict`, `router.latency.p95` and a `router.auth_mismatch_count`.

deploy-bot: deploy completed: console-router v2 -> canary 2/50 tenants

maya: action items: sam to prototype routing-first + add header; alex to implement fallback logic + metrics; irene to run SLO perf baseline & test regression sets; luca to run threat-model and sign-off on invalidation hooks. 2-week checkin on progress.

sam: ack

luca: ack, will write threat notes and an invalidation plan.

maya: thanks all :thumbsup:
